# Normalise line endings. Authored on Windows, CI is green on Linux (README),
# so text is stored LF in the repository and checked out native.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows batch files must keep CRLF. Some cmd.exe constructs (notably a
# multi-line GOTO label or a trailing argument) misparse with bare LF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Shell scripts must be LF or the shebang line carries a stray CR and the
# interpreter is not found.
*.sh  text eol=lf

# Explicitly text
*.py   text
*.md   text
*.txt  text
*.json text
*.html text
*.yml  text
*.yaml text

# Captured console output, kept byte-exact and NOT normalised.
#
# These are records of a run, not source. Each carries genuinely mixed
# endings -- CLEANUP.txt is 2 CRLF and 35 bare LF -- because a .bat wrote
# some lines and a Python step appended others to the same handle. Under
# `text=auto eol=lf` the clean filter strips the surviving CRLF while the
# committed blob keeps it, so the file reports a permanent 2-line diff that
# no checkout can settle. Measured, after chasing a wrong guess first: there
# are no \r\r\n sequences in any of them, so this is not the usual wmic
# double-CR artefact -- it is simple interleaving.
#
# A record that cannot round-trip is not a record. -text stores them exactly
# as captured.
CLEANUP.txt       -text
FREE_RAM.txt      -text
LEAN_MEASURE*.txt -text
diag_out.txt      -text
pc_report.txt     -text

# Binary: never diff, never normalise
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.msi  binary
*.bin  binary
*.db   binary
*.zip  binary
